The attached image illustrates the active transport and cellular targeting of Nano-11 nanoparticles in draining lymph nodes following intramuscular injection. Panel A and B show that Nano-11-containing myeloid cells peak at 3.5% of total myeloid cells in the lymph node on Day 2, significantly decreasing by Day 7. Panel C demonstrates that MigDCs consistently internalize the largest proportion of Nano-11 (60-80% of Nano-11+ cells). Panel D further indicates that MigDCs exhibit the highest mean fluorescence intensity (MFI) for Nano-11 uptake, highlighting their efficiency in acquiring the nanoparticles. These data collectively underscore Nano-11's ability to target critical antigen-presenting cells.

The image can significantly contribute to vaccine development by providing crucial insights into the in vivo behavior and cellular targeting of Nano-11 as an adjuvant. The demonstrated efficient uptake by migratory dendritic cells (MigDCs) and their subsequent transport to draining lymph nodes highlight Nano-11's potential to enhance antigen presentation, a critical step for inducing robust adaptive immunity. This understanding allows for the rational design of adjuvant-delivery systems that optimize cellular targeting and kinetics, leading to more effective and targeted vaccine formulations for improved protective responses.